.TH NAN 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
nan, nanf, nanl \- generate a quiet NaN
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double nan(const char *" tagp );
.br
.BI "float nanf(const char *" tagp );
.br
.BI "long double nanl(const char *" tagp );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR nan ()
family of functions returns a quiet NaN
.RB ( "Not a Number" )
of the appropriate floating-point type.  These functions provide a
portable, expression-context way to obtain a NaN value without relying
on the
.B NAN
macro or on any particular bit pattern.
.PP
The argument
.I tagp
is a pointer to a null-terminated string interpreted as an
n-char-sequence whose contents
.I may
be used to select an implementation-defined NaN payload (the
significand bits below the quiet-NaN indicator).  Per ISO C99
\(sc7.12.11.2 the call
.BI nan( tagp )
is equivalent to
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
strtod("NAN(\fItagp\fP)", (char **)NULL);
.EE
.RE
.PP
when
.I tagp
points to a valid n-char-sequence, and equivalent to
.B strtod("NAN()", NULL)
when
.I tagp
points to an empty string.  The mapping from
.I tagp
to payload bits is implementation-defined; if the implementation does
not support NaNs of that form, the function returns zero.
.PP
The Substrate libm currently
.I ignores
.I tagp
and unconditionally returns the same generic quiet NaN as the
.B NAN
macro.  This is conforming \(em the standard requires only that the
returned value be a quiet NaN of the correct type, and that the
payload be implementation-defined \(em but it does mean that distinct
.I tagp
arguments produce indistinguishable results.  Code that needs to
discriminate NaN payloads must not depend on these functions to encode
information.
.PP
The
.BR nanf ()
variant returns a quiet
.B float
NaN; the
.BR nanl ()
variant returns a quiet
.B "long double"
NaN.  All three functions are total: they are defined for every
argument value, including
.I "tagp == NULL"
(treated as if the empty string were supplied).  No floating-point
exception is raised; in particular
.B FE_INVALID
is never raised, because the result is a
.I quiet
NaN, not a signaling NaN.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR nan (),
.BR nanf (),
and
.BR nanl ()
return a quiet NaN of type
.BR double ,
.BR float ,
and
.BR "long double"
respectively.  The returned value satisfies
.BI isnan( x )
and
.B "fpclassify(x) == FP_NAN"
for any
.I x
holding the result.
.PP
No errors are defined for these functions.  They never set
.I errno
and never raise a floating-point exception.  In Substrate the result
is always a quiet NaN with a generic (zero) payload, regardless of
.IR tagp .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR nan (),
.BR nanf (),
.BR nanl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR nan (),
.BR nanf (),
and
.BR nanl ()
conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The interpretation of
.I tagp
follows ISO C99 \(sc7.12.11.2 and IEC 60559:1989 (IEEE\ 754); the
specific payload bits selected are implementation-defined.  Substrate
treats the
.I tagp
mapping as a no-op and always returns the canonical quiet NaN of the
target format.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program obtains a quiet NaN from
.BR nan (),
verifies that it classifies as a NaN, and shows that arithmetic on a
NaN propagates without raising an exception:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = nan("");
    double y;

    if (isnan(x))
        printf("nan(\e"\e") produced a NaN\en");

    /* Arithmetic with a NaN propagates the NaN. */
    y = x + 1.0;
    printf("nan(\e"\e") + 1.0 isnan = %d\en", isnan(y));

    /* All comparisons against NaN are false (except !=). */
    printf("(x == x) = %d   (x != x) = %d\en",
           x == x, x != x);

    /* The tag string is ignored on Substrate; both calls
       yield indistinguishable quiet NaNs. */
    printf("isnan(nan(\e"foo\e")) = %d\en", isnan(nan("foo")));
    printf("isnan(nanf(\e"\e"))   = %d\en", isnan(nanf("")));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
nan("") produced a NaN
nan("") + 1.0 isnan = 1
(x == x) = 0   (x != x) = 1
isnan(nan("foo")) = 1
isnan(nanf(""))   = 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Note that
.B "x == x"
is false when
.I x
is a NaN: this is the canonical idiom for detecting NaN in code that
predates
.BR isnan (3),
and it remains correct under IEC 60559 ordered comparison rules.
